> No, it isn't; the ss in -ness and -less are perfectly natural, since
> they derive from ME -nesse and -lesse, and -ful from -fulle. When the -e
> was no longer pronounced, it was eventually dropped from the spelling,
> and the second -l in -full as well; but if you drop the second s in
> -ness, the pronunciation changes. Compare beatless music with Beatles
> music, for example.
>
> > > 2/ One would really expect a careful person to be full of care, just
> > > like a careless person has less care than others...
> >
> > I know. But then again, why doesn't one write the word "full" with a
> > single "l"?
>
> Again, because that would change the natural pronunciation. It would
> look like it should be [f^l] rather than [ful]. You can drop the second
> l in -full, since as a suffix it often actually _is_ pronounced [f^l].

